          %p
          #RepresentabilityIsAMereProposition.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              %strong (representability is a mere proposition)
              %br/
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+category"} univalent category
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mere+proposition"} mere proposition
          This is Theorem 9.5.9 in the
          = succeed "." do
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HoTT+book"} HoTT book
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              By definition “F is representable” is just the fiber of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi y
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{y}_0
              over
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi y
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{y}_0
              is an embedding by Corollary 9.5.7 in the HoTT book (see
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p The central point about examples of representable functors is:
          %p
            %em Representable functors are ubiquitous
            \.
          %p
            To a fair extent,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            is all about representable functors and the other
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+constructions"} universal constructions
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+extensions"} Kan extensions
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"} adjoint functors
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
          %p
            Listing examples of representable functors in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            is much like listing examples of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral"} integral
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analysis"} analysis
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
            for more).
          %p Keeping that in mind, we do list some special cases and special classes of examples that are useful to know. But any list is necessarily wildly incomplete.
